<!-- social.dsx — the comment thread under an episode, and the SAFETY SET that App Store
     guideline 1.2 requires of any app carrying user-generated content.

     WHY THE SAFETY SET IS NOT OPTIONAL. 1.2 asks for FOUR things, and an app that ships a
     comment field without them is rejected on that alone:
       (a) a method for FILTERING objectionable material from being posted,
       (b) a mechanism to REPORT offensive content, with timely responses,
       (c) the ability to BLOCK abusive users,
       (d) PUBLISHED CONTACT INFORMATION so a reader can reach the developer.
     This document owns (a), (b) and (c). (d) is `supportUrl` in App.json `consts`, rendered
     by `Components/parts/ContactRow.dsx` and by `<LegalLinks>` on the account screen — one
     config key, one degradation, no second plane.

     WORTH KNOWING: comments are NOT a category convention. ReelShort, DramaBox and GoodShort
     have no comment affordance at all (docs/feature-parity.md, correction 5). So this feature
     puts the template AHEAD of the reference, and the bar it has to clear is Apple's rather
     than the category's.

     ═══ THE OWNERSHIP DECISIONS, AND WHY THEY ARE NOT THE SAME WORD ═══════════════════════

     `comment`  public-read — anyone reads a thread; only the author writes their own row
                (the own-write half is server/policies.local.sql, the §6.25 addendum).
     `block`    OWNER — nobody but you needs to know whom you blocked, and RLS enforces it.
                The generated owner policies are exactly right; no addendum.
     `report`   public-read — and this one is a TRADE, made deliberately and named here.

     A report has two readers: the operator (the moderation queue) and the thread read (which
     counts flags to auto-hide a comment the community has flagged). A declared action NEVER
     runs as service_role — `runAs` sets `set local role authenticated` for every user-scope
     query regardless of the token role claim (PLAN.md §6.7) — so an OWNER-scoped report table
     is readable by its reporter and by nobody else, ever, through the declared plane. The
     operator queue would be unreachable and the flag tally would always read 1.
     So `report` is all-read, and what keeps a viewer out of the queue is the ROUTE GATEWAY:
     `/social/reports` carries `reach=""`, which host.ts admits only for a service-role
     identity or DSX_INTERNAL_KEY and answers 404 to everyone else. Said plainly: RLS does not
     protect report rows here, the gateway does. The rows hold a comment id, a reason code, a
     snapshot of already-public text and the reporter subject (a random UUID, not a
     credential). The day `auth="role:operator"` (§6.2) and a service-scoped declared read
     exist, this becomes owner-read plus one operator read and the trade disappears.

     WHAT THIS DOCUMENT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT SHIP:
       · a `hidden` boolean on `comment` set by an operator. Writes to a public-read table are
         service-only, and §6.7 means no declared action can perform one — so the column would
         be decoration nobody can move. The community threshold below does the same job with a
         mechanism that actually runs.
       · a `state` (open/actioned) column on `report`, for the same reason: the operator cannot
         write it. Resolution belongs to whatever moderation tool an adopter wires the queue to.
       · a word list pretending to be moderation. See `postComment`. -->
<server>
  <head>
    <!-- `author_id` IS `owner_id`, DECLARED, and it exists for exactly one reason: a viewer
         must be able to delete their account and take their comments with them.
         `list` filters accept DECLARED FIELDS ONLY — repo.ts drops anything else in silence,
         so `filters: { owner_id: … }` is not a query, it is a full table scan wearing a filter.
         On an OWNER table that does not matter (RLS has already narrowed the rows to yours);
         on a public-read table it is the whole problem, and it is why account deletion could
         not empty this one. MEASURED before this field existed: a viewer who owned nothing at
         all looped /viewer/delete eight times, "removed" two rows a pass and never finished,
         because the sweep was listing EVERY comment in the deployment and deleting none.
         Nothing writes this column. `server/policies.local.sql` gives it `default auth.uid()`,
         the same way owner_id is filled, so it is assigned by Postgres from the verified
         subject and a client cannot set it (postComment builds its own values object). -->
    <entity as="comment" ownership="public-read">
      <field as="show" type="uuid"/>
      <field as="episode" type="uuid"/>
      <field as="body" type="text"/>
      <field as="author_id" type="uuid"/>
      <index on="episode"/>
      <index on="author_id"/>
    </entity>

    <!-- THE REPORT. One row per (reporter, target), enforced by a unique index in
         server/policies.local.sql — the same lock every other once-only guard in this backend
         uses, because a declared action has no transaction seam (§6.38) and a read-then-write
         guard races.
         `kind` exists so this is ONE report seam for the whole app rather than two: a comment
         report carries `target` (the comment row) and an AD report carries `subject` (the
         creative path), which is the seam `Components/parts/AdGate.dsx` emits its `reported`
         event for under guideline 2.5.18. -->
    <entity as="report" ownership="public-read">
      <field as="kind" type="text"/>        <!-- comment | ad -->
      <field as="target" type="uuid"/>      <!-- the reported comment row, when kind is comment -->
      <field as="subject" type="text"/>     <!-- a target with no row of its own: an ad creative -->
      <field as="episode" type="uuid"/>
      <field as="show" type="uuid"/>
      <field as="reason" type="text"/>      <!-- spam | harassment | sexual | violence | other -->
      <field as="note" type="text"/>
      <field as="snapshot" type="text"/>    <!-- the reported text, so the queue survives a delete -->
      <!-- the same declared-owner column, for the same reason: see `comment.author_id` -->
      <field as="reporter_id" type="uuid"/>
      <index on="target"/>
      <index on="episode"/>
      <index on="reporter_id"/>
    </entity>

    <!-- THE BLOCK. `subject` is the blocked author owner_id — the RLS subject, which is what
         a comment row carries and therefore the only stable handle a blocker can name.
         `handle` is the derived display name, stored so the unblock list can say who without
         a second read. Owner-scoped: your block list is yours. -->
    <entity as="block" ownership="owner">
      <field as="subject" type="uuid"/>
      <field as="handle" type="text"/>
      <index on="subject"/>
    </entity>

    <!-- The viewer's inbox. Notices are broadcast rows (ownership=public-read, declared in
         admin.dsx and written only through the operator surface); this read shapes them
         for the inbox — banner kinds first, newest first, the audience segment carried so
         a client can filter locally when it knows the viewer is VIP. Read-state is the
         CLIENT's memory (a lastSeen timestamp), because a per-viewer read receipt is a
         write amplification this demo does not need. -->
    <action as="listNotices">
      const rows = await dsx.module.data.notice.list({ limit: 100 })
      if (!rows.ok) { return { rows: [] } }
      const shaped = rows.data
        .sort((a, b) => (a.created_at < b.created_at ? 1 : -1))
        .map(n => ({ id: n.id, title: n.title, message: n.message, segment: n.segment, kind: n.kind, at: n.created_at }))
      return { rows: shaped }
    </action>

    <!-- ══ THE THREAD READ — where FILTER and BLOCK are actually enforced ══════════════════
         Three module calls, all bounded: the comments, the caller BLOCK list, the episode
         REPORT tally. The route is auth="none" so a guest still sees the conversation; a
         guest simply has no blocks (an owner-policy table yields nothing to the anon role,
         which is a fact of the RLS and not a branch here).

         WHY THIS IS SERVER-SIDE AND NOT CLIENT-SIDE. A blocked author filtered in markup is
         still in the payload, in the SSR html and in `curl` — the same defect the paywall had
         (audit G1: the client hid the locked episodes and the API handed out every URL). A
         viewer who blocks someone is telling the app "do not show me this person", and the
         only implementation of that promise which cannot leak is one where the bytes never
         leave the origin. It costs one extra bounded read.

         THE AUTHOR NAME IS DERIVED, NEVER STORED FROM THE CLIENT. The row used to be written
         with `author: 'You'`, so every thread rendered as one person talking to themselves
         (audit M1). The token contract carries only `sub` (docs/auth.md), and a server action
         has no identity plane — but every row it reads carries `owner_id`, which Postgres
         assigned from the verified subject and no client can forge. So the display name is
         computed from it here, at read time, for everyone consistently. It is not a real
         profile name; the upgrade is a `name` claim on the token or a `profile` entity that
         this read joins, and both change this one expression. -->
    <action as="listComments" inputs="episode, level">
      if (episode == null || episode === '') { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'episode is required' } }
      // HIDE_AT is the community threshold. One report must NOT hide a comment for everyone —
      // that hands any single account a censor button — so the hide is two-stage: it is
      // immediate and personal for the reporter (the client drops the row the moment the
      // report is filed) and collective only at HIDE_AT distinct reporters, which the unique
      // index on (owner_id, target) is what makes "distinct" true.
      const HIDE_AT = 3
      const strict = level === 'strict'
      const rows = await dsx.module.data.comment.list({ filters: { episode: episode }, limit: 100 })
      if (!rows.ok) { return { count: 0, rows: [], blocked: 0, hidden: 0, threshold: HIDE_AT, strict: strict } }

      // the caller own block list. Empty for a guest, by RLS rather than by an if.
      const mine = await dsx.module.data.block.list({ limit: 100 })
      let blockedSubs = []
      if (mine.ok) {
        for (const b of mine.data) { blockedSubs = blockedSubs.concat([b.subject == null ? '' : String(b.subject)]) }
      }

      // the episode flag tally. A bracket WRITE-BACK on a variable key is the supported
      // spelling; a bracket-read mutation would be a silent no-op (AGENTS.md).
      const flagged = await dsx.module.data.report.list({ filters: { episode: episode }, limit: 100 })
      const tally = {}
      if (flagged.ok) {
        for (const f of flagged.data) {
          const k = f.target == null ? '' : String(f.target)
          if (k !== '') { tally[k] = (tally[k] == null ? 0 : tally[k]) + 1 }
        }
      }

      const out = []
      let blockedOut = 0
      let hiddenOut = 0
      for (const c of rows.data) {
        const oid = c.owner_id == null ? '' : String(c.owner_id)
        const n = tally[c.id] == null ? 0 : tally[c.id]
        if (blockedSubs.includes(oid) && oid !== '') { blockedOut = blockedOut + 1 }
        else if (n >= HIDE_AT) { hiddenOut = hiddenOut + 1 }
        else if (strict && n > 0) { hiddenOut = hiddenOut + 1 }
        else {
          // Grow the handle from a non-numeric anchor: JSE `+` is total arithmetic, so a
          // string built the other way round becomes a number (AGENTS.md, PLAN.md).
          const tail = oid === '' ? '' : oid.slice(0, 6).toUpperCase()
          const who = tail === '' ? 'Viewer' : 'Viewer ' + tail
          out.push({ id: c.id, owner: oid, author: who, body: c.body, flags: n, at: c.created_at })
        }
      }
      return { count: out.length, rows: out, blocked: blockedOut, hidden: hiddenOut, threshold: HIDE_AT, strict: strict }
    </action>

    <!-- ══ THE POST-TIME FILTER — 1.2(a), and exactly what it is ═══════════════════════════
         WHAT IT DOES. It refuses the shapes that are objectively unwanted under a drama
         episode and can be recognised WITHOUT reading meaning: links, phone-length digit runs,
         shouting, and keyboard mashing. Each refusal names itself, so the viewer can fix the
         comment instead of guessing.

         WHAT IT DOES NOT DO, said plainly because a template that implied otherwise would be
         teaching a lie to everyone who forks it: it does not detect hate speech, harassment,
         threats, sexual content or slurs. A word list cannot — one accent character walks
         through it and the same list fires on the innocent use of the same string — so this
         file ships none. The semantic half of moderation is carried by the two mechanisms
         that do not need to read meaning: the per-viewer BLOCK and the community FLAG
         threshold in `listComments`, plus the operator queue.

         WHAT A PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT ADDS, precisely: one classifier call between the checks
         below and the create. Declare the host on the action (`egress="api.example-moderation.com"`)
         and its key as a secret (`<secret as="moderationKey"/>` in this head), POST the text,
         and throw `{ reason: 'invalid', message: … }` on a positive. That is the whole change;
         nothing else in this document or in the player moves. -->
    <action as="postComment" inputs="show, episode, body">
      const text = body == null ? '' : String(body).trim()
      if (text === '') { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'Say something first.' } }
      if (text.length > 500) { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'Comments are capped at 500 characters.' } }
      const low = text.toLowerCase()
      const linkish = ['http://', 'https://', 'www.', '.com', '.net', '.org', '.ru/', 't.me/', 'wa.me/', 'bit.ly']
      for (const probe of linkish) {
        if (low.includes(probe)) { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'Links are not allowed in comments.' } }
      }
      // ONE bounded pass over at most 500 characters, reading two structural signals: the
      // longest run of one repeated character, and the longest run of digits with the
      // separators a phone number is written with. The loop ceiling is 50000 (ACTION_LOOP_CAP).
      let prev = ''
      let run = 0
      let maxRun = 0
      let digits = 0
      let maxDigits = 0
      for (const ch of text.split('')) {
        if (ch === prev) { run = run + 1 } else { prev = ch; run = 1 }
        if (run > maxRun) { maxRun = run }
        const code = ch.charCodeAt(0)
        if (code >= 48 && code <= 57) { digits = digits + 1 }
        else if (ch === ' ' || ch === '-' || ch === '.' || ch === '(' || ch === ')' || ch === '+') { digits = digits }
        else { digits = 0 }
        if (digits > maxDigits) { maxDigits = digits }
      }
      if (maxDigits >= 7) { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'Phone numbers and contact details are not allowed in comments.' } }
      if (maxRun >= 8) { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'That reads as repeated characters rather than a comment.' } }
      if (text.length >= 24 && text === text.toUpperCase() && low !== text) {
        throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'Please do not post in all capitals.' }
      }
      const made = await dsx.module.data.comment.create({ show: show, episode: episode, body: text })
      if (!made.ok) { throw { reason: 'conflict', message: 'Could not post that.' } }
      // The create returns the row Postgres wrote, so the caller subject is knowable HERE and
      // nowhere else in a server action — the same derivation `listComments` performs, so an
      // optimistic row and a refetched one carry the same name.
      const oid = made.data.owner_id == null ? '' : String(made.data.owner_id)
      const tail = oid === '' ? '' : oid.slice(0, 6).toUpperCase()
      return { id: made.data.id, body: text, owner: oid, author: tail === '' ? 'Viewer' : 'Viewer ' + tail, at: made.data.created_at }
    </action>

    <!-- ══ REPORT — 1.2(b) ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         IDEMPOTENT PER (reporter, target), and the lock is the unique index rather than a
         read: a report table is all-read, so a scoped "have I already reported this?" query
         is not expressible — `filters` accepts declared fields only and `owner_id` is not one
         (repo.ts drops an undeclared filter silently).
         So the create goes FIRST, exactly as every other guard in this backend now does. When
         it is refused, one bounded read decides which refusal it was: rows already exist on
         this target, so a duplicate is the explanation and the viewer is told the honest
         thing (it is already reported); NO rows exist, so a unique violation is impossible and
         the write was refused by POLICY — which is an adopter who has not applied
         server/policies.local.sql, and it must be said out loud rather than swallowed as
         success. The one case this cannot separate — someone else reported it AND my insert
         was policy-refused — reports as a duplicate; it is unreachable in a deployment where
         the addendum is applied, which the first report on any comment proves. -->
    <action as="reportComment" inputs="target, reason, note">
      if (target == null || target === '') { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'a comment is required' } }
      const reasons = ['spam', 'harassment', 'sexual', 'violence', 'other']
      const why = reason == null || reason === '' ? 'other' : String(reason)
      if (!reasons.includes(why)) { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'unknown report reason' } }
      const row = await dsx.module.data.comment.get({ id: target })
      if (!row.ok || row.data == null) { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'that comment no longer exists' } }
      // The snapshot, the episode and the show come from the ROW, never from the caller: a
      // queue that trusted the reporter for the text it shows an operator could be used to
      // put words in someone else mouth.
      const said = row.data.body == null ? '' : String(row.data.body).slice(0, 300)
      const values = {
        kind: 'comment', target: target, reason: why, snapshot: said,
        note: note == null ? '' : String(note).trim().slice(0, 300)
      }
      // NEVER a `field: null` in create values (AGENTS.md) — omit the key instead. A literal
      // path assignment on a local dict is the supported spelling.
      if (row.data.episode != null) { values.episode = row.data.episode }
      if (row.data.show != null) { values.show = row.data.show }
      const made = await dsx.module.data.report.create(values)
      if (made.ok) { return { filed: true, duplicate: false } }
      const already = await dsx.module.data.report.list({ filters: { target: target }, limit: 100 })
      if (already.ok && already.data.length > 0) { return { filed: true, duplicate: true } }
      throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'The report could not be filed. If this is a fresh deployment, apply server/policies.local.sql — dsx_report needs its owner-insert policy.' }
    </action>

    <!-- THE AD REPORT, same seam. `Components/parts/AdGate.dsx` raises `reported` with the
         lane and the creative for guideline 2.5.18; `Components/Rewards.dsx` catches it and
         today only acknowledges the viewer, because no route existed. This is that route.
         HAND-OFF, one line, in a file this document does not own: `adReported` in
         Rewards.dsx should POST { lane, creative } here and report what the server said. -->
    <action as="reportAd" inputs="lane, creative, note">
      const what = creative == null || creative === '' ? String(lane == null ? '' : lane) : String(creative)
      if (what === '') { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'a creative is required' } }
      const named = what.slice(0, 300)
      const values = {
        kind: 'ad', subject: named, reason: 'other',
        snapshot: 'lane: ' + String(lane == null ? 'house' : lane),
        note: note == null ? '' : String(note).trim().slice(0, 300)
      }
      const made = await dsx.module.data.report.create(values)
      if (made.ok) { return { filed: true, duplicate: false } }
      const already = await dsx.module.data.report.list({ filters: { subject: named }, limit: 1 })
      if (already.ok && already.data.length > 0) { return { filed: true, duplicate: true } }
      throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'The report could not be filed. If this is a fresh deployment, apply server/policies.local.sql — dsx_report needs its owner-insert policy.' }
    </action>

    <!-- ══ BLOCK — 1.2(c) ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         KEYED ON THE COMMENT, resolved to the author here. The client holds the author
         subject already (it needs it to render "You" on its own rows), but taking the comment
         id means the server decides who the author is, which is the same rule the snapshot
         above follows. Idempotent through unique (owner_id, subject). -->
    <action as="blockAuthor" inputs="comment">
      if (comment == null || comment === '') { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'a comment is required' } }
      const row = await dsx.module.data.comment.get({ id: comment })
      if (!row.ok || row.data == null) { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'that comment no longer exists' } }
      const who = row.data.owner_id == null ? '' : String(row.data.owner_id)
      if (who === '') { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'that comment has no author to block' } }
      const tail = who.slice(0, 6).toUpperCase()
      const made = await dsx.module.data.block.create({ subject: who, handle: 'Viewer ' + tail })
      if (made.ok) { return { blocked: true, subject: who, handle: 'Viewer ' + tail } }
      // owner-scoped, so this read is the caller own list and the answer is exact
      const mine = await dsx.module.data.block.list({ filters: { subject: who }, limit: 1 })
      if (mine.ok && mine.data.length > 0) { return { blocked: true, subject: who, handle: mine.data[0].handle } }
      throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'The block could not be saved.' }
    </action>

    <action as="unblockAuthor" inputs="subject">
      if (subject == null || subject === '') { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'a subject is required' } }
      const mine = await dsx.module.data.block.list({ filters: { subject: subject }, limit: 1 })
      if (!mine.ok || mine.data.length === 0) { return { blocked: false } }
      // same rule as deleteComment: `ok` survives a row RLS filtered away, `data` does not
      const gone = await dsx.module.data.block.delete({ id: mine.data[0].id })
      if (!gone.ok || gone.data == null) { throw { reason: 'unavailable', message: 'The block could not be removed.' } }
      return { blocked: false, subject: subject }
    </action>

    <action as="listBlocks">
      const mine = await dsx.module.data.block.list({ limit: 100 })
      if (!mine.ok) { return { count: 0, rows: [] } }
      const out = []
      for (const b of mine.data) { out.push({ id: b.id, subject: b.subject, handle: b.handle, at: b.created_at }) }
      return { count: out.length, rows: out }
    </action>

    <!-- DELETE YOUR OWN COMMENT. The owner-delete policy has existed since the §6.25 addendum
         landed; nothing exposed it. RLS is the whole guard — this action names no owner and
         cannot, so a delete of somebody else row is refused by Postgres rather than by an if
         that could be forgotten. -->
    <action as="deleteComment" inputs="comment">
      if (comment == null || comment === '') { throw { reason: 'invalid', message: 'a comment is required' } }
      const gone = await dsx.module.data.comment.delete({ id: comment })
      // A DELETE FILTERED AWAY BY RLS IS `ok`, AND THAT IS THE TRAP. The statement is
      // `delete … where id = $1 returning id`, so a row the policy hides simply matches
      // nothing: no error, no throw, `ok` true — and the transport answers `null` because it
      // returns `rows[0] ?? null` for every non-list op. Measured here before this line
      // existed: viewer A deleted viewer B comment and the API said `{deleted: true}` while the
      // row was still in the thread. The DATA, not the flag, is what says a row went.
      if (!gone.ok || gone.data == null) { throw { reason: 'forbidden', message: 'You can only delete your own comment.' } }
      return { deleted: true, id: comment }
    </action>

    <!-- ══ THE OPERATOR QUEUE ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Behind `reach=""`: service-role identity or DSX_INTERNAL_KEY, 404 for everyone else.
         Newest first, bounded at 100, with a per-target count so the operator sees which
         comment several people flagged. There is deliberately no "mark handled" verb — a
         declared action cannot write a public-read table (§6.7), so a `state` column would be
         a control nobody can move. Resolution lives in whatever moderation tool the queue is
         drained into, and `deleteComment` above is the only in-app remedy that actually runs.
         NO `<tool>` ROW, on purpose: createMcpFace checks `auth === "required"` and a non-null
         identity and has no notion of `reach` (§6.84), so exposing this queue over /mcp would
         hand every authenticated caller the thing the gateway just refused them.

         `threshold` rides the payload so the queue can say which rows the COMMUNITY FOLD has
         already acted on: at HIDE_AT distinct reporters `listComments` withholds the comment
         from every standard reader, and that is the only remedy in this app that actually
         runs. An operator cannot delete a comment they do not own — measured against this
         origin, `POST /social/comment/delete` with the service-role token answers 403 "You
         can only delete your own comment", because a declared action carries no service
         authority (§6.7). The Manage screen says so rather than drawing a button that 403s. -->
    <action as="listReports">
      const rows = await dsx.module.data.report.list({ limit: 100 })
      if (!rows.ok) { return { count: 0, rows: [], capped: false, threshold: 3 } }
      // A BRACKET KEY CONTAINING A DOT IS WRITTEN AS A NESTED PATH, silently. This tally was
      // `tally[k] = …` keyed on `target ?? subject`, and a comment target (a UUID) counted
      // correctly while EVERY ad report came back `flags: null` — because an ad subject is a
      // creative path and every creative path ends in `.mp4`, so `tally["/promo/house-ad.mp4"]`
      // wrote `tally["/promo/house-ad"]["mp4"]` and read back nothing. Measured on a live
      // origin: dotted key -> null, dot-free key -> 1, same code path (PLAN.md §6.93).
      // The fix is to stop keying a dict on caller data at all. n is bounded at 100 here, so
      // the pairwise count is at most 10k comparisons and has no encoding hazard whatever.
      const out = rows.data
        .sort((a, b) => (a.created_at < b.created_at ? 1 : -1))
        .map(r => ({
          id: r.id, kind: r.kind, target: r.target, subject: r.subject,
          episode: r.episode, show: r.show, reason: r.reason, note: r.note,
          snapshot: r.snapshot, reporter: r.owner_id, at: r.created_at,
          flags: rows.data.filter(o => (o.target == null ? o.subject : o.target) === (r.target == null ? r.subject : r.target)).length
        }))
      return { count: out.length, rows: out, capped: rows.data.length >= 100, threshold: 3 }
    </action>
  </head>

  <!-- RATE CEILINGS.
         · comments 120/m — a public read the player opens per episode, and re-opens when the
           viewer changes the filter level. An authenticated caller is bucketed by `sub`; an
           anonymous one shares a bucket per address (scripts/serve.mjs supplies clientAddress).
         · notices   60/m — a public read of one small table.
         · comment   10/m — the write. Nobody types ten distinct comments a minute.
         · report / block  20/m — sized to the SWEEP and not to the intent: a viewer cleaning
           up one bad thread files several in a row, and a ceiling that locks them out halfway
           through moderating their own feed is a ceiling that defeats the feature it protects.
         · blocks    60/m — a read the safety panel refreshes.
         · reports  600/m — the operator lane, which bulk-drains. -->
  <route method="GET"  path="/social/comments/:episode" action="listComments"  auth="none"     rate="120/m"/>
  <route method="GET"  path="/social/notices"           action="listNotices"   auth="none"     rate="60/m"/>
  <route method="GET"  path="/social/blocks"            action="listBlocks"    auth="required" rate="60/m"/>
  <route method="POST" path="/social/comment"           action="postComment"   auth="required" rate="10/m"/>
  <route method="POST" path="/social/comment/delete"    action="deleteComment" auth="required" rate="20/m"/>
  <route method="POST" path="/social/report"            action="reportComment" auth="required" rate="20/m"/>
  <route method="POST" path="/social/report/ad"         action="reportAd"      auth="required" rate="20/m"/>
  <route method="POST" path="/social/block"             action="blockAuthor"   auth="required" rate="20/m"/>
  <route method="POST" path="/social/unblock"           action="unblockAuthor" auth="required" rate="20/m"/>
  <route method="POST" path="/social/reports"           action="listReports"   auth="required" reach="" rate="600/m"/>
</server>
